@packages/sigilc/turtle.sigil from SigilTurtleInput import { AssertionValidation }
@packages/core/src/design-input.sigil from SigilDesignInput import { ResolvedDesignInput }

component SigilWorldClosure {
  goal {
    Enrich one asserted world with finite compiler-owned egglog laws.
  }

  interface {
    IndependentComparison {
      Compare finite Design obligations with independently saturated Implementation
      facts in a third fixed eqval instance. Design facts never enter Implementation
      closure or its matching tables. A missing positive relation cannot satisfy a
      prohibition. Return Drift for positive disagreement, Converged for unresolved
      evidence, and Closed only for Coherent Design, fresh complete selected inputs
      and satisfied obligations. Disjoint Design leaves Implementation state unset. A
      current contradiction takes precedence over unrelated missing inputs. Reject
      attempts to compare a Design closure as Implementation or to reuse closure from
      a different compiled runtime.
    }

    DesignObligations {
      Derive required interpretation from the ResolvedDesignInput physical
      inventory. A
      complete structured proposition can interpret a unit; a model assertion
      claiming completeness cannot. Keep Design interpretation obligations separate
      from requirements on code. Inspect missing fulfillment after closure. Return
      Disjoint for contradictions, Loose for unresolved required meaning, and
      Coherent when neither remains. Operational errors return no state.

      Lower provides to a positive provides obligation, requires to availability of
      provides, excludes to a uses prohibition, and required propositions to their
      exact predicate and expected value. Latency budgets require bounded
      Implementation latency. Required states need an owner, and exclusive ownership
      prohibits multiple independently observed owners. Missing extra owners cannot
      prove exclusive ownership complete. Other descriptive metadata and relations do
      not automatically impose code obligations.
    }

    IsolatedClosure {
      Each saturation creates a fresh EGraph containing only that world's assertions
      accepted through AssertionValidation. Derive dependency reachability,
      delegated capabilities, structured
      propositions, contradictions, minimum distance and maximum risk. Return sorted
      fixed tables with bounded rule and source witnesses.
    }

    RuntimeIdentity {
      Fingerprint the actual compiled eqval, lowering code and pinned runtime
      dependencies. Reports identify the engine that produced their evidence.
    }
  }

  constraints {

    StructuralProvenance {
      Carry schema 2 Tag, selection, reference, link, Facet-owner, contract-role and
      source-range provenance beside saturation inputs and results. This structural
      companion adds no implication law, obligation-satisfaction shortcut, behavioral
      equivalence, or change to the existing finite behavior algebra.
    }
    IsolatedClosure {
      Workers cannot supply rules or fill compiler-owned tables. Keep raw conflicting
      values so minimum and maximum merges cannot hide contradictions. Use bounded
      tuple witnesses without concatenated semantic IDs or recursively expanding
      proof strings. Reject unsupported numeric path sums. Bound input rows, runtime
      rows, iterations and elapsed time. Check runtime limits between default egglog
      iterations and before returning. Limit failure returns no completed closure or
      semantic verdict.
    }
  }

  cases {
    IsolatedClosure {
      Linking A dependsOn B in one object with B dependsOn C in another derives A
      reachable C without modifying either object. Saturating a world separately does
      not carry those facts into it. Positive cost cycles terminate through minimum
      merge; reachable risk propagates through maximum merge.
    }
  }
}
